Background and Motivation: Our lab's recent projects on student motivation and learning analytics have built expertise in measuring self-regulated learning (SRL) and mining LMS/homework logs, and this project applies that foundation to test how online homework feedback shapes students' SRL trajectories and equity-related outcomes in introductory psychology.  
Research Questions: 1) How do different types of online homework feedback (answer-only, explanatory, metacognitive prompts, mastery-adaptive) affect trajectories of students’ self-regulated learning (SRL) behaviors across a semester; 2) To what extent do SRL changes mediate the effects of feedback on academic performance (homework, exams, final grade); 3) Do feedback effects and SRL trajectories differ by equity-relevant markers (race/ethnicity, Pell eligibility, first-generation status).  
Study Design and Data Plan: Primary—cluster-randomized trial assigning introductory-psychology course sections to one feedback condition for the semester, collecting automated LMS/homework logs (time-on-task, spacing, attempts, hint use), pre/post SRL surveys, and course performance; analyze with mixed-effects growth models, mediation, and moderation by equity markers.  
Backup—within-course staggered rollout/crossover (introduce feedback types in different units or weeks) to enable within-student comparisons if section-level randomization is infeasible, with the same automated and survey measures for comparability.  
Timeline and Feasibility:
- Months 0–2: finalize research questions/measures, build feedback content and analytics pipeline, set up secure data storage and access controls, submit IRB application;  
- Month 3: pilot in one section, collect pilot logs/surveys, refine procedures and materials;  
- Months 4–7: run semester study (implement feedback conditions, collect LMS logs, administer surveys, gather course performance data);  
- Months 8–9: clean and de-identify data, run preliminary analyses, prepare internal report and draft seed-grant materials for the PI.
